# Signal Bus

## Overview

When several Agents need the same Signal, sending it to each one by name ties
the sender to every receiver. A Signal bus removes that tie: the sender
publishes to a named bus, and each Agent subscribes to the Signal types it
wants.

Two Plugins connect an Agent to `Jido.Signal.Bus`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
plugins do
  plugin(Jido.Plugin.Bus.Manager, config: %{name: :orders})
  plugin(Jido.Plugin.Bus.Client, config: %{bus: :orders, path: "order.**"})
end
```

`Bus.Manager` starts a bus and ties its lifetime to the Agent. `Bus.Client`
subscribes the Agent to one path, and each matching Signal runs a normal Turn.

In this Livebook you build three Agents around one `:orders` bus:

- a ledger that owns the bus and records orders;
- a checkout Agent that publishes orders after it commits; and
- an archive with a durable subscription that catches up on missed records.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Wait Helper

Bus delivery is asynchronous: a published Signal reaches its subscribers
after `publish/2` returns. This helper polls a condition every 20 ms for up to
3 seconds, so the cells below don't depend on timing.

```elixir
defmodule BusGuide.Wait do
  def until(fun, timeout \\ 3_000) do
    deadline = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + timeout
    loop(fun, deadline)
  end

  defp loop(fun, deadline) do
    case fun.() do
      {:ok, value} ->
        {:ok, value}

      _not_yet ->
        if System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) >= deadline do
          :timeout
        else
          Process.sleep(20)
          loop(fun, deadline)
        end
    end
  end
end
```

## Define The Ledger

The ledger's Action records each order ID. It also keeps every Signal ID it
has processed, which the durable subscription later in this guide needs.

```elixir
defmodule BusGuide.RecordOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "record_order",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: id}, %{agent_state: state, signal: signal}) do
    if signal.id in state.seen do
      {:ok, state}
    else
      {:ok, %{state | seen: state.seen ++ [signal.id], orders: state.orders ++ [id]}}
    end
  end
end
```

The ledger owns the `:orders` bus and subscribes to it. Declare `Manager`
before `Client`: Plugins start in declaration order, and the bus must exist
before the Client subscribes.

```elixir
defmodule BusGuide.Ledger do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "ledger"

  state do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        orders: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([]),
        seen: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])
      })
    )
  end

  plugins do
    plugin(Jido.Plugin.Bus.Manager, config: %{name: :orders})

    plugin(Jido.Plugin.Bus.Client,
      config: %{bus: :orders, path: "order.**", retry_delay_ms: 50}
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/signal-bus")
    route "order.placed", BusGuide.RecordOrder do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

`path: "order.**"` matches every type that starts with `order.`. The path
selects what the Client delivers. The routes still select what runs, so a
delivered Signal with no matching route fails its Turn.

## Start The Ledger

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, ledger} = Jido.start_agent(BusGuide.Ledger, id: "ledger")

Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(ledger)
```

The result is `:ok`. `await_ready/2` returns after the Client has subscribed.
Until then, Turns return `{:error, :not_ready}`.

The bus is registered in the Agent's Jido instance:

```elixir
{:ok, bus} = Jido.Signal.Bus.whereis(:orders, jido: Jido.default_instance())

is_pid(bus)
```

The result is `true`.

## Publish A Signal

Any process can publish. The bus records the Signal, then delivers it to each
matching subscription.

```elixir
signal = Jido.Signal.new!("order.placed", %{order_id: "A-1"}, source: "/guides/signal-bus")
{:ok, [recorded]} = Jido.Signal.Bus.publish(bus, [signal])

recorded.type
```

The result is `"order.placed"`. Each recorded Signal also has a `cursor`: its
position in the bus log.

Wait for the ledger to commit the order:

```elixir
BusGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(ledger).state.orders do
    [] -> :wait
    orders -> {:ok, orders}
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, ["A-1"]}`. The publisher didn't reference the ledger.

## Publish From An Agent

An Action publishes with an `emit` Directive and a bus dispatch target.
`{:bus, target: :orders}` sends the Signal to the bus named `:orders`.

```elixir
defmodule BusGuide.PlaceOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "place_order",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: id}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    placed =
      Jido.Signal.new!("order.placed", %{order_id: id}, source: "/guides/signal-bus/checkout")

    {:ok, %{state | placed: state.placed + 1},
     [Jido.Agent.Directive.emit(placed, {:bus, target: :orders})]}
  end
end

defmodule BusGuide.Checkout do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "checkout"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{placed: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/signal-bus/checkout")
    route "checkout.place", BusGuide.PlaceOrder, as: :place do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

The target has no `jido:` option. The Agent Server adds its own Jido instance,
so the lookup finds the ledger's bus.

```elixir
{:ok, checkout} = Jido.start_agent(BusGuide.Checkout, id: "checkout")
place = BusGuide.Checkout.place_signal!(%{order_id: "A-2"})
{:ok, _committed, [_emit]} = Jido.AgentServer.call(checkout, place)

BusGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(ledger).state.orders do
    ["A-1", "A-2"] = orders -> {:ok, orders}
    _ -> :wait
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, ["A-1", "A-2"]}`.

The publish is a Directive, so it runs after checkout commits `placed: 1`. If
the checkout Turn fails, nothing reaches the bus.

## Replay The Log

The bus keeps every record. `replay/2` reads them back by path:

```elixir
{:ok, records} = Jido.Signal.Bus.replay(bus, "order.**")

Enum.map(records, & &1.signal.data)
```

The result is `[%{order_id: "A-1"}, %{order_id: "A-2"}]`.

## Durable Subscriptions

A normal subscription casts each record to the Agent. If the Agent isn't
running when a record arrives, it never gets that record.

A durable subscription has a stable ID, and the bus keeps a cursor for that
ID. With `durable:` set, the Client:

- calls the Agent instead of casting;
- receives the next record only after it acknowledges the current one; and
- acknowledges a record only after its Turn commits.

```elixir
defmodule BusGuide.Archive do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "archive"

  state do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        orders: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([]),
        seen: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])
      })
    )
  end

  plugins do
    plugin(Jido.Plugin.Bus.Client,
      config: %{
        bus: :orders,
        path: "order.**",
        durable: "order-archive",
        start_from: :origin,
        retry_delay_ms: 50
      }
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/signal-bus/archive")
    route "order.placed", BusGuide.RecordOrder do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

`start_from: :origin` reads from the start of the log the first time the
durable ID subscribes. Without it, a new durable subscription starts at the
end of the log.

Start the archive after both orders exist:

```elixir
{:ok, archive} = Jido.start_agent(BusGuide.Archive, id: "archive")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(archive)

BusGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(archive).state.orders do
    ["A-1", "A-2"] = orders -> {:ok, orders}
    _ -> :wait
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, ["A-1", "A-2"]}`.

Stop the archive, publish while it's down, then start it again:

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("archive")

missed = Jido.Signal.new!("order.placed", %{order_id: "A-3"}, source: "/guides/signal-bus")
{:ok, _records} = Jido.Signal.Bus.publish(bus, [missed])

{:ok, archive} = Jido.start_agent(BusGuide.Archive, id: "archive")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(archive)

BusGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
  case Jido.AgentServer.agent(archive).state.orders do
    [] -> :wait
    orders -> {:ok, orders}
  end
end)
```

The result is `{:ok, ["A-3"]}`. The bus kept the cursor, so the new archive
received `A-3`. It doesn't have `A-1` or `A-2`: those lived in the stopped
activation, and this Agent has no persistence. The cursor is stored in the
bus. Agent state is stored only if you configure persistence for the Agent.

### At-Least-Once Delivery

When a Turn fails, the Client retries the same record after `retry_delay_ms`.
Later records for that durable ID wait until it succeeds, so order holds. A
Turn can also commit and then lose its acknowledgement, so the Agent can
receive the same Signal twice.

Make durable handlers idempotent. `RecordOrder` does this by checking
`signal.id` against `seen`: a repeat commits with no change, and the Client
acknowledges it.

## Client Options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bus` | required | Name of the bus to subscribe to. |
| `path` | `"**"` | Signal type pattern to receive. |
| `durable` | `nil` | Stable durable subscription ID. |
| `start_from` | end of log | Where a new durable subscription starts. Requires `durable`. |
| `retry_delay_ms` | `1_000` | Delay before retrying a failed delivery, acknowledgement, or reconnect. |
| `timeout` | `5_000` | Call timeout, in ms, for one durable delivery. |
| `jido` | the Agent's instance | Bus lookup scope. `nil` looks up a global bus. |

The Client doesn't reject unknown options. A misspelled key such as
`retry_delay: 50` is ignored and the default applies. The Client monitors the
bus and reconnects after `retry_delay_ms` if the bus stops.

## Manager Options

The Manager passes its config to `Jido.Signal.Bus.child_spec/1` and sets two
defaults:

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | the Agent ID | Bus name used by publishers and Clients. |
| `jido` | the Agent's instance | Registry scope. `nil` registers a global bus. |

Other options, such as `store:`, go to `Jido.Signal.Bus` unchanged. The
default memory store loses its records and cursors when the bus or its Agent
stops. Set `store:` to a persistent `Jido.Signal.Bus.Store` if they must
survive a restart.

## Topology Buses

A topology can also declare a bus between member Agents. `Jido.Plugin.Bus.DSL`
adds a `signal_bus` section, and connections route to it with `bus(:name)`.
See [Connect Agent Outputs](connect-agent-outputs.livemd).

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("archive")
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("checkout")
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("ledger")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

The `:orders` bus stops with the ledger.

## Summary

- `Bus.Manager` starts a bus that stops with its Agent.
- `Bus.Client` turns matching bus Signals into normal Turns.
- `emit(signal, {:bus, target: name})` publishes after commit.
- A durable Client acknowledges a record only after its Turn commits.
- Durable delivery is at least once. Make handlers idempotent.
- The bus stores the cursor. The Agent stores its state only with
  persistence.

